<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaska</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaska was an ancient Indian grammarian and author of the Nirukta, the oldest surviving treatise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> etymology and the interpretation of Vedic words. Usually dated to around the 5th century BCE, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>, he discussed how words derive from verbal roots. In passing he notes that the Kambojas used a distinct word for going, often taken as evidence of their Iranian speech.</p></div>
